From: Shirogane Tsumugi <artoriapendragon123@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] sunxi: spi: move the read sample point with the bus speed

SPI_TCR has two bits controlling when the master samples MISO and the
driver leaves both at zero regardless of speed. That is the right setting
for the middle of the range, which is why it has never been noticed, but
it does not hold at either end.

Past roughly 60MHz the round trip -- SCLK propagating to the slave and its
data coming back -- no longer fits inside the setup time, and SDC (bit 11)
has to delay the sample point by half an SCLK cycle. Below roughly 24MHz
the default delay is itself the problem and SDM (bit 13) selects plain
sampling instead. F1C200s manual 7.3.7.2 documents both; the thresholds
are the ones the BSP driver applies in sunxi_spi_mode_check().

This is a prerequisite for raising the bus speed on suniv: the CDR2
divider hangs off the 200MHz AHB, so the reachable rates are 100/50/33MHz
and nothing in between. Asking for anything under 100 lands on 50, which
sits in the no-adjustment window -- but 100MHz needs SDC.

The bits live in the sun6i register layout only. sun4i has no equivalent,
and leaving its bits[] entries unset makes both masks 0, so the
read-modify-write is a no-op there.

Signed-off-by: Shirogane Tsumugi <artoriapendragon123@gmail.com>

--- a/drivers/spi/spi-sunxi.c
+++ b/drivers/spi/spi-sunxi.c
@@ -83,6 +83,10 @@
 #define SUN4I_SPI_MIN_RATE		3000
 #define SUN4I_SPI_TIMEOUT_US		1000000
 
+/* Speeds at which the read sample point has to move, as used by the BSP */
+#define SUN4I_SPI_HIGH_RATE		60000000
+#define SUN4I_SPI_LOW_RATE		24000000
+
 #define SPI_REG(priv, reg)		((priv)->base + \
 					(priv)->variant->regs[reg])
 #define SPI_BIT(priv, bit)		((priv)->variant->bits[bit])
@@ -115,6 +119,9 @@
 	SPI_TCR_XCH,
 	SPI_TCR_CS_MANUAL,
 	SPI_TCR_CS_LEVEL,
+	/* Sample point control; sun4i has no equivalent and leaves these 0 */
+	SPI_TCR_SDC,
+	SPI_TCR_SDM,
 	SPI_FCR_TF_RST,
 	SPI_FCR_RF_RST,
 	SPI_FSR_RF_CNT_MASK,
@@ -527,6 +534,21 @@
 	priv->freq = speed;
 	writel(reg, SPI_REG(priv, SPI_CCR));
 
+	/*
+	 * Past a point the round trip of SCLK going out and the slave's data
+	 * coming back no longer fits in the setup time, so the sample point
+	 * has to move half a cycle later; slow buses want it moved the other
+	 * way. The thresholds are the ones the BSP driver uses. On sun4i both
+	 * bits are 0, which leaves the register untouched.
+	 */
+	reg = readl(SPI_REG(priv, SPI_TCR));
+	reg &= ~(SPI_BIT(priv, SPI_TCR_SDC) | SPI_BIT(priv, SPI_TCR_SDM));
+	if (speed >= SUN4I_SPI_HIGH_RATE)
+		reg |= SPI_BIT(priv, SPI_TCR_SDC);
+	else if (speed <= SUN4I_SPI_LOW_RATE)
+		reg |= SPI_BIT(priv, SPI_TCR_SDM);
+	writel(reg, SPI_REG(priv, SPI_TCR));
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
@@ -667,6 +689,8 @@
 	[SPI_TCR_CS_MASK]	= 0x30,
 	[SPI_TCR_CS_MANUAL]	= BIT(6),
 	[SPI_TCR_CS_LEVEL]	= BIT(7),
+	[SPI_TCR_SDC]		= BIT(11),
+	[SPI_TCR_SDM]		= BIT(13),
 	[SPI_TCR_XCH]		= BIT(31),
 	[SPI_FCR_RF_RST]	= BIT(15),
 	[SPI_FCR_TF_RST]	= BIT(31),
